INT. CURLING RINK - PRE-DAWN

A tin-roofed rink in a Manitoba grain town, the ice a long pale sheet scored by a thousand rocks. Breath hangs in the cold. Somewhere a compressor thrums like a heart beneath the floor.

RAINE KOWALCHUK, 40s, the skip, kneels at the hack with her broom across her knees, studying the far house as though it owed her money. TEODORA BRZEZINSKI, 60s, the coach, leans on the boards in a parka the colour of canned peaches, thermos steaming. PRIYA NAIR, 20s, the lead, sweeps a pebble from the lane with great ceremony, while WALT DUNPHY, 70, the second, lowers himself onto a bench with a groan like a gate, and SUNDAY ADEYEMI, 30s, the third, rests her chin on her broom handle and watches the three of them as if counting something.

Everything is calm. Even the compressor seems to hush.

TEODORA
Draw weight's fine. Ice is keen. You'll hit the button on the last end like you've done ten thousand times.

RAINE
We're not at the last end.

TEODORA
We will be.

WALT
Eleven-nine, they said. Eleven-nine and we're the underdogs. Never been an underdog. I was always the old dog.

PRIYA
You're still the old dog, Walt.

WALT
Language.

SUNDAY
Raine. You haven't said a word about the match.

RAINE
Didn't think I needed to.

SUNDAY
You always have a word about the match.

RAINE
Fine. I have a word. After today I'm not skipping.

TEODORA
You're saying you want to throw last stones. I can arrange that.

RAINE
I'm saying I'm done, Teo. Not skipping. Not anything. After today I'm gone. Winnipeg. The job came through in September and I sat on it. I didn't want you carrying it into the match.

PRIYA
Gone gone?

RAINE
Gone gone.

WALT
Forty-one years I've known your father and he never told me.

RAINE
He doesn't know either. You're the first.

SUNDAY
Why us? Why now?

RAINE
Because Teo's been lying to me about the ice for three weeks, and I figured if we're all confessing.

TEODORA
It is perfectly good ice.

RAINE
It's pebbled with the wrong water, Teo. You've been using the well water since the municipal line froze. I can read it from the hack.

TEODORA
It plays true.

RAINE
It plays true. It plays true for us. It won't for them. That's why you wanted the old stones thrown first. That's why you wanted me skipping one more match.

TEODORA
(after a long pause)
I wanted to win once more. Is that a crime in Manitoba?

Walt laughs, once, helplessly. Priya covers her mouth. Sunday doesn't move.

Raine rises, stiff in the knees, and walks the length of the sheet to the far house. She sets down the broom. Takes off a glove. Presses her bare palm flat to the ice and holds it there while the others watch.

She stands and lifts a hand: the thumbs-up of a skip who knows the line.
